// @test: a recursive `our<T>` field reclaims without unbounded compiler recursion (#427 defect 5)
// @description: `struct L { v<Int>, next<our<L>> }` crashed the compiler with SIGSEGV and no
//   diagnostic: releaseOurControlBlock began its payload reclaim with a FRESH `visited` set, so
//   the reclaim expansion for a recursive type never terminated -- reclaim(L) -> release the
//   `next` handle -> reclaim(L) -> ... until the stack died inside LLVM's value symbol table.
//   The payload reclaim now shares the caller's set, so the expansion terminates; a two-node
//   chain is built through both the literal field and a field assignment, then read back.
// @category: ownership, struct
// @expect-output: chain 1->2
// @expect-return: 0

share(all)
struct L { v<Int>, next<our<L>> }

main()<Int> -> {
    n2<our<L>> = our(L { v: 2 })
    n1<our<L>> = our(L { v: 1, next: n2 })
    n1.next = n2
    println("chain " + n1.v.to_string() + "->" + n1.next.v.to_string())
    return 0
}
